course: books
lesson: 11
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter VI, part 1: The footmen and the Duchess's kitchen
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Nine-inch Alice watches a footman with a face like a fish hand a giant letter to a footman with a face like a frog: the Queen invites the Duchess to play croquet. When they bow, their curly wigs get tangled, and Alice has to run off to hide her laughter.
explain: When she knocks, the Frog-Footman explains, very seriously and very stupidly, why knocking is useless, and doesn't even blink when a plate flies out and just misses his nose.
explain: Alice gives up on him and walks straight in, into a smoky kitchen where the air is so full of pepper that everyone sneezes: the Duchess, nursing a howling baby, a cook stirring soup, and a big cat grinning from ear to ear.
explain: The cook starts throwing pans and dishes; the Duchess ignores them, insults Alice, and when Alice talks about the earth's axis, shouts "chop off her head!"
explain: Then the Duchess sings the baby a horrible lullaby about beating a boy when he sneezes, shaking him at every line: noisy, rude and crazy, a kitchen where nobody behaves the way grown-ups should.
fact: A footman is a servant who opens doors and carries messages; livery is the uniform servants of a great house wore; both footmen have powdered hair curled all over their heads.
fact: Alice thinks one is a footman "because he was in livery: otherwise, judging by his face only, she would have called him a fish"; the other has "a round face, and large eyes like a frog".
fact: The Fish-Footman brings a letter "nearly as large as himself": "For the Duchess. An invitation from the Queen to play croquet."
fact: The Frog-Footman repeats it "only changing the order of the words a little": "From the Queen. An invitation for the Duchess to play croquet."; croquet is a lawn game where you hit balls through hoops with a mallet.
fact: When they both bow low, "their curls got entangled together", and Alice laughs so much she runs back into the wood.
fact: When Alice comes back, the Frog-Footman is sitting on the ground near the door, "staring stupidly up into the sky"; from inside comes "a constant howling and sneezing", and now and then a great crash.
fact: The Footman says there's no use knocking for two reasons: "First, because I'm on the same side of the door as you are; secondly, because they're making such a noise inside, no one could possibly hear you."
fact: A plate comes "skimming out, straight at the Footman's head", grazes his nose (just scrapes it) and breaks, and he goes on talking "exactly as if nothing had happened": "I shall sit here till tomorrow—or next day, maybe."
fact: Alice mutters "It's really dreadful, the way all the creatures argue. It's enough to drive one crazy!", calls him "perfectly idiotic", and goes in.
fact: The kitchen is full of smoke; the Duchess sits on a three-legged stool nursing a baby, and the cook stirs a large cauldron (a big cooking pot) of soup.
fact: "There's certainly too much pepper in that soup!", says Alice; only the cook and the cat do not sneeze.
fact: The Duchess says "It's a Cheshire cat, and that's why. Pig!" (shouting "Pig!" at the baby) and tells Alice "You don't know much, and that's a fact"; Alice says "I didn't know that cats could grin"; Cheshire is a county in England, and "to grin like a Cheshire cat" was an old saying.
fact: The cook throws fire-irons, saucepans, plates and dishes at the Duchess and the baby; the Duchess takes no notice of them, even when they hit her, and Alice cries "Oh, there goes his precious nose!"; this grinning cat is nothing like Alice's Dinah, who, she told the Mouse, sits "purring so nicely by the fire".
fact: The Duchess says "If everybody minded their own business, the world would go round a deal faster than it does", and Alice, "very glad to get an opportunity of showing off a little of her knowledge", explains the earth takes twenty-four hours to turn on its axis (which is true).
fact: The Duchess answers "Talking of axes, chop off her head!", a pun on axis and axes (chopping tools), and later "I never could abide figures!" ("abide" = stand, "figures" = numbers).
fact: The Duchess's lullaby says "Speak roughly to your little boy, And beat him when he sneezes: He only does it to annoy, Because he knows it teases"; it is a silly, shocking twist of a gentle old poem, and hitting children is cruel and wrong.
fact: Old words in today's English: "uncivil" = rude, "most of 'em" = most of them, "a deal faster" = much faster, "severely" = harshly, "in an agony of terror" = terribly frightened.
exercise: Why did Alice think the first servant was a footman? | He was in livery | He had a big letter | He knocked loudly | He wore a gold crown
exercise: What would Alice have called him, judging by his face only? | A fish | A green frog | A fat pig | A grinning cat
exercise: What did the second footman look like? | A round face and large eyes like a frog | A long nose and sharp teeth like a fox | Big ears and a fluffy tail like a rabbit | Feathers and a hooked beak like an eagle
exercise: What is livery? | The uniform that servants of a great house wore | A large letter sealed with wax for a duchess | The kitchen where servants cook the soup | A white powder that servants put on their hair to look smart
exercise: How big was the letter? | Nearly as large as the Fish-Footman himself | About as small as a postage stamp | Exactly the size of the little golden key | As large as the whole of the Duchess's house
exercise: What was the letter? | An invitation from the Queen to play croquet | A bill from the cook for too much pepper | A note from the Rabbit about his gloves | A letter from Alice's sister to bring her home
exercise: What is croquet? | A lawn game where you hit balls through hoops | A card game played at the kitchen table | A dance done by two footmen together | A kind of thick soup made with a great deal of pepper in it
exercise: What happened when the two footmen bowed? | Their curls got tangled together | They bumped their heads hard | Their wigs fell off on the grass | They fell over into the mud
exercise: Why did Alice run back into the wood? | So they would not hear her laughing | Because the footmen chased her | Because a plate flew at her head | So she could eat some more of the mushroom she held
exercise: What was the Frog-Footman doing when Alice came back? | Sitting on the ground staring up at the sky | Running off to the Queen with an answer | Knocking on the door again and again | Sweeping up the pieces of broken plates lying by the door
exercise: What was the Footman's first reason it was no use knocking? | He was on the same side of the door as Alice | The door had no knocker on it at all | Nobody lived inside the house any more | The Duchess had gone out to play croquet
exercise: What was his second reason? | The noise inside was too loud to hear a knock | The Duchess was fast asleep and must not wake | The door was made of glass and would break | Knocking was not allowed on a Thursday
exercise: What noises came from inside the house? | Howling, sneezing and crashing | Singing, laughing and clapping | Snoring, ticking and purring | Hammering, sawing and drilling
exercise: What flew out of the door at the Footman's head? | A large plate | A heavy frying-pan | A large plum cake | A big black kettle
exercise: How did the Footman react when the plate grazed his nose? | He went on exactly as if nothing had happened | He shouted and ran into the wood to hide | He threw the plate back into the kitchen | He fell over backwards and started to cry
exercise: Here "grazed" means... | Just scraped | Ate the grass | Missed by miles | Broke it badly
exercise: Who was nursing a baby in the kitchen? | The Duchess | The old cook | The Queen of Hearts | Mary Ann, the Rabbit's maid
exercise: What was the cook doing? | Stirring a large cauldron of soup | Washing up the dishes in a big sink | Baking little cakes marked "EAT ME" | Sweeping the floor with a broom
exercise: Why was everyone sneezing? | There was too much pepper in the air | They all had very bad colds | The cat's fur was everywhere | The smoke from the fire was full of dust
exercise: Who did NOT sneeze in the kitchen? | The cook and the cat | The Duchess and the baby | Alice and the Footman | The baby and the cat
exercise: Why did the Duchess say her cat grinned? | "It's a Cheshire cat, and that's why" | "It has just eaten a mouse for its tea" | "It is laughing at your silly face" | "Because it knows a secret about you"
exercise: Who was the Duchess shouting "Pig!" at? | The baby | Alice herself | The old cook | The grinning cat
exercise: What did the cook throw at the Duchess and the baby? | Fire-irons, saucepans, plates and dishes | Pepper, salt, sugar and flour | Cakes, comfits, pebbles and keys | Shoes, boots, kid gloves and a large fan
exercise: What did the Duchess say would happen if everybody minded their own business? | The world would go round a deal faster | The soup would taste much better | Everyone would stop sneezing | The baby would go to sleep at last and stop howling
exercise: What did the Duchess shout when Alice mentioned the earth's axis? | "chop off her head!" | "Speak English!" | "Keep your temper!" | "A likely story indeed!"
exercise: What is the pun in "Talking of axes"? | Axis (the line the earth spins on) sounds like axes (chopping tools) | Axes are a kind of pepper that the cook puts into the soup | The earth really is turned round by a giant axe each day | The Duchess thinks the cat is called Axis, after the earth
exercise: Here "I never could abide figures" means... | I never could stand numbers | I never could draw people | I never could count sheep | I never could find my way home in the dark
exercise: In the lullaby, what does the singer say to do when the boy sneezes? | Beat him | Kiss him gently | Feed him porridge | Hug him tight
exercise: Thinking back: who was afraid the Duchess would be "savage", or even have him executed? | The White Rabbit | The Mouse | The Caterpillar | Bill the Lizard, who went down the chimney
exercise: Thinking back to chapter V: how tall had Alice made herself before coming near this house? | Nine inches | About four feet | Three inches | Over nine feet
test: Ruby goes: "The footman let Alice in really politely." Put her right. | He talked nonsense, and Alice just opened the door herself | He bowed and showed her straight in to see the Duchess | He told her to come back tomorrow, so she went away | He knocked for her, and the cook opened the door at once
test: Zeke, Gen Alpha: "That Frog-Footman is so mid, he just sits there, no rizz at all." ("mid" = boring, "rizz" = charm.) What does he say he'll do? | Sit there till tomorrow, or next day, maybe | Go and fetch the Duchess straight away for Alice | Play croquet with the Queen this very afternoon | Run off into the wood to find the Fish-Footman
test: Why is the Footman's first reason for not knocking funny? | Knocking is for someone on the other side of a door, and he's outside too | Footmen are never allowed to answer the door in Wonderland at all | Knocking is rude, and Alice should have rung the bell instead of it | He is a frog, and frogs cannot hear any knocking on wooden doors
test: What would have happened if Alice had waited for the Footman to let her in? | She might have waited for days and days | He would have let her in after a minute | The Duchess would have come out to her | The Queen would have sent her a letter
test: Nan from Liverpool: "That cook's a right headcase, chuckin' pans at a baby!" ("headcase" = crazy person, "chuckin'" = throwing.) How did the Duchess react to the flying pans? | She took no notice, even when they hit her | She threw them straight back at the cook | She hid under the table with the baby | She told the cook off and sent her out
test: True or false: the Duchess is kind to Alice. | False: she says "You don't know much, and that's a fact" | True: she gives Alice a cup of soup and a seat | True: she thanks Alice for saving the baby's nose | False: she never says a single word to Alice
test: Why does Alice talk about the earth turning on its axis? | She is glad of a chance to show off her knowledge | The Duchess asked her a question about the earth | She wants to teach the baby some science lessons | The cook had asked her how long the big pot of soup should boil
test: Chloe, sarky: "Oh great, a lullaby about beating kids, so relaxing." What should we think about the Duchess's song? | It is a silly, shocking song; hitting children is cruel and wrong | It is good advice that all parents should follow at home | It is a song that real babies love to hear at bedtime | It is a lesson about why we should never eat pepper
test: Why does the song say the boy sneezes? | "He only does it to annoy, Because he knows it teases" | He has caught a bad cold from swimming in the pool | The cat's fur has tickled his nose all day long | He wants to wake the Duchess up from her nap
test: Jimmy from Dublin: "Sure the whole house is mad, yer man's lookin' at the sky and yer woman's singin'." ("yer man" / "yer woman" = that man / that woman.) Who is "looking at the sky"? | The Frog-Footman | The Fish-Footman | The cook | The cat
test: Looking back to chapter I: Alice once played croquet against herself and cheated. What game does the Queen's letter invite the Duchess to? | Croquet | Cricket | Chess | Tennis
test: Looking back to chapter V: what did Alice say about all the creatures, which she repeats in other words here? | That the creatures are touchy and hard to talk to | That they are all kind and very helpful | That they all love cats more than dogs | That they all want to be three inches tall
test: Looking back to chapter I: what else did Alice say about the earth while falling? | That the centre was about four thousand miles down | That the earth was flat like a plate on a table | That the earth turned only once a year round | That the earth was made of cheese and pepper
test: How is Alice's cat Dinah different from the cat in this kitchen? | Dinah purrs by the fire; this cat grins from ear to ear | Dinah is a Cheshire cat that also grins all day | Dinah sneezes all day long because of pepper | Dinah is enormous and lives in the Duchess's house
test: ★★★ Fish-Footman: "For the Duchess. An invitation from the Queen". Frog-Footman: "From the Queen. An invitation for the Duchess". Which two little words swapped places? | "for" and "from" | "Queen" and "Duchess" | "An" and "the" | "to" and "play"
test: ★★★ Read carefully: the Footman says knocking might make sense "if you were inside". Why? | Then the door would be between them, and he could let her out | Then the noise inside would be quieter for him to hear | Then the Duchess would be able to open it for herself | Then the plate could not hit his nose as it flew out
test: ★★★ Alice says the earth turns on its axis in "Twenty-four hours, I think; or is it twelve?" Which is right? | Twenty-four hours | Twelve hours | Six hours | Forty-eight hours
exam: Identify the communication delivered by the Fish-Footman to the Frog-Footman. | An invitation from the Queen to the Duchess to play croquet | A summons from the Duchess to the Queen to attend at once | An order to the cook to put less pepper in the soup | A letter from the White Rabbit concerning his gloves
exam: Ascertain the cause of the universal sneezing within the kitchen. | An excess of pepper in the air | A prevalence of feline fur | A severe outbreak of influenza | Smoke from a damp chimney
exam: It is given that the protagonist referred to the earth's axis. Determine the Duchess's response thereto. | An order that the protagonist's head be chopped off | A request for the protagonist to repeat the figures | An expression of profound gratitude for the lesson | A refusal to speak further until the soup was served
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the conduct of the Duchess towards the missiles thrown by the cook. | She took no notice of them, even upon being struck | She returned each article to the cook forthwith | She sheltered the infant beneath the table | She dismissed the cook from her service at once
